#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""AI Project Dashboard: keep our server-side work from being silently overwritten.

Why this exists: the dashboard's code was first deployed with `deploy/deploy.sh` from another computer (a Mac), which makes the
server's code IDENTICAL to that computer's copy and deletes whatever else is on the server. Work done directly on the server (the
model picker, project notes, approval cards, …) is not in that copy, so the next deploy from it would erase it — and a database
migration that the other copy doesn't know would stop that deploy half way. The dashboard's source is not on GitHub either.

What this tool does (runs as root, lives OUTSIDE the app folder so a deploy can't remove it):
  save SOURCE "message" [--force]
                          checkpoint: SOURCE (the complete source, tests included) must equal what is deployed; it is committed to a
                          git repo in /var/lib/aidash-source and remembered as the last known good state. Run it after EVERY deploy.
                          It says what changed since the last checkpoint and REFUSES (unless --force) when files or database
                          migrations that the last checkpoint had are gone — that is what a deploy from an older copy looks like.
  check                   (every 5 minutes, by ai-dashboard-guard.timer) compare the deployed code with the checkpoint, the database's
                          migration head with the code's migrations, the saved git copy, and the login page; on a problem raise ONE
                          alert per kind of problem (the dashboard's bell, Telegram when it is set up) and write
                          /etc/ai-dashboard/code-guard.json for the Settings page.
  restore [REV] [--dry-run]  put a checkpoint's code back (backups of the code and the database first, packages installed, only this
                          app is restarted, automatic rollback if it does not come up). Refuses when it would leave the database
                          ahead of the code.
  status                  print the saved state.
It never changes the app by itself: an alert is a question to a person, not an order."""
